1
00:00:00,028 --> 00:00:01,289
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,642 --> 00:00:06,026
I'm Ravi Ray, and welcome to this
session on Beyond Basic RAG: Better

3
00:00:06,028 --> 00:00:08,955
Context for More Reliable Enterprise AI.

4
00:00:09,283 --> 00:00:12,898
We spend a lot of time on prompt
engineering, and rightly so.

5
00:00:13,222 --> 00:00:16,579
But once we move into real
enterprise applications, we

6
00:00:16,579 --> 00:00:18,021
find something interesting.

7
00:00:18,367 --> 00:00:22,052
A beautifully written prompt can't
rescue a system if the information it's

8
00:00:22,066 --> 00:00:25,003
given is old, incomplete, or just wrong.

9
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:29,853
So today I want to focus on the other
half of the equation: how we design

10
00:00:29,869 --> 00:00:31,560
the knowledge and retrieval layer.

11
00:00:31,835 --> 00:00:36,379
I'll share a practical vendor-neutral
framework, look at the engineering

12
00:00:36,383 --> 00:00:41,733
challenges that are still open, and finish
with concrete steps your team can apply.

13
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:44,383
Let's begin with a simple question.

14
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:49,695
Why do two applications using the same
language model and almost the same

15
00:00:49,695 --> 00:00:51,900
prompt produce very different answers?

16
00:00:52,206 --> 00:00:56,069
Often, the difference is the
context each one retrieves.

17
00:00:56,381 --> 00:01:00,599
Imagine an employee asking
about the current travel policy.

18
00:01:01,390 --> 00:01:06,292
One system retrieves last year's policy,
another finds the latest approved version.

19
00:01:06,645 --> 00:01:09,081
Same prompt, very different outcome.

20
00:01:09,408 --> 00:01:13,562
For enterprise AI, I'd put
the emphasis on three things:

21
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:17,722
knowledge freshness, information
governance, and retrieval precision.

22
00:01:18,346 --> 00:01:23,494
We need the right information at the
right time, and we must never expose

23
00:01:23,504 --> 00:01:26,599
documents the user isn't allowed to see.

24
00:01:26,903 --> 00:01:30,119
Good retrieval isn't a
replacement for good prompting.

25
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:32,047
The two work together.

26
00:01:32,389 --> 00:01:38,746
Now, you said the two work together,
but in production, retrieval quality is

27
00:01:38,773 --> 00:01:40,686
often the harder engineering problem.

28
00:01:41,686 --> 00:01:47,755
Retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG,
has moved well beyond a prototype feature.

29
00:01:48,073 --> 00:01:52,307
For many enterprise AI products, it's
becoming part of the core architecture.

30
00:01:52,624 --> 00:01:55,308
We should think about it the
way we think about databases,

31
00:01:55,336 --> 00:01:57,465
APIs, and identity systems.

32
00:01:57,773 --> 00:02:01,174
It needs reliable ingestion,
predictable performance, clear

33
00:02:01,176 --> 00:02:03,453
ownership, and operational monitoring.

34
00:02:03,784 --> 00:02:09,287
In a prototype, we might upload
a few PDFs, create embeddings,

35
00:02:09,954 --> 00:02:11,736
and see useful answers.

36
00:02:12,048 --> 00:02:14,006
That's a very good way to start.

37
00:02:14,322 --> 00:02:19,198
But enterprise environments bring
changing content, multiple repositories,

38
00:02:19,499 --> 00:02:25,643
different permissions, and users who
expect consistent answers every day.

39
00:02:25,951 --> 00:02:29,718
So the question is no longer just can
the model retrieve something relevant?

40
00:02:30,039 --> 00:02:34,202
It's can the whole system retrieve
the right evidence consistently,

41
00:02:34,212 --> 00:02:35,621
securely, and at scale?

42
00:02:36,621 --> 00:02:39,626
To organize this problem, I
use a framework with five axes.

43
00:02:39,924 --> 00:02:45,116
The first is ingestion, how we parse,
normalize, and chunk diverse content.

44
00:02:45,391 --> 00:02:50,325
The second is indexing, how we
make that content searchable using

45
00:02:50,325 --> 00:02:53,411
dense, sparse, or hybrid approaches.

46
00:02:53,702 --> 00:02:58,309
The third is retrieval composition,
how we rewrite queries, combine search

47
00:02:58,309 --> 00:03:00,022
results, and re-rank candidates.

48
00:03:00,314 --> 00:03:06,127
The fourth is grounding, how we connect
every important answer back to evidence.

49
00:03:06,446 --> 00:03:10,672
And the fifth is governance: permissions,
provenance, and auditability.

50
00:03:10,978 --> 00:03:13,699
These aren't five isolated boxes.

51
00:03:14,016 --> 00:03:15,908
Each one affects the others.

52
00:03:16,227 --> 00:03:20,009
A great re-ranker can't repair a missing
table from the original document,

53
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:25,241
and a very accurate search is still
unsafe if access controls are ignored.

54
00:03:25,926 --> 00:03:31,047
So this framework lets us review the
complete retrieval system instead of

55
00:03:31,054 --> 00:03:34,130
optimizing one component in isolation.

56
00:03:35,130 --> 00:03:37,816
This framework comes from a
comparative analysis of roughly

57
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:42,478
twenty-five implementations across
academic research, open source

58
00:03:42,489 --> 00:03:44,762
frameworks, and enterprise platforms.

59
00:03:45,083 --> 00:03:50,849
The objective wasn't to rank vendors or to
claim that one library solves everything.

60
00:03:51,168 --> 00:03:54,401
Instead, I looked for recurring patterns.

61
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:57,025
What's common in research prototypes?

62
00:03:57,322 --> 00:03:59,518
What changes when those
systems reach production?

63
00:03:59,881 --> 00:04:05,007
And which requirements show up again
and again in enterprise deployments?

64
00:04:05,277 --> 00:04:06,323
Here's what I found.

65
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:11,354
Production complexity is often outside
the retrieval algorithm itself.

66
00:04:11,683 --> 00:04:16,774
Permissions, lineage, updates, and
evaluation become first-class concerns.

67
00:04:17,066 --> 00:04:21,034
That's why I present this as a
vendor-neutral engineering lens.

68
00:04:21,351 --> 00:04:24,380
The specific tools will change.

69
00:04:24,675 --> 00:04:27,075
The questions we need
to ask will stay useful.

70
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:31,443
Now, let's start with ingestion,
because many retrieval

71
00:04:31,444 --> 00:04:32,951
problems actually begin here.

72
00:04:33,261 --> 00:04:38,741
Imagine a technical PDF containing
text, diagrams, tables, and footnotes.

73
00:04:39,016 --> 00:04:42,100
If the parser loses a column heading,
a later search may return text

74
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,948
that looks relevant, but no longer
means what the author intended.

75
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:47,027
That's not a prompt problem.

76
00:04:47,372 --> 00:04:49,969
It's not really a vector database problem.

77
00:04:50,306 --> 00:04:51,797
It's an ingestion problem.

78
00:04:52,123 --> 00:04:56,797
We need to handle format diversity:
PDFs, HTML, presentations,

79
00:04:57,082 --> 00:04:58,744
code, and structured records.

80
00:04:59,014 --> 00:05:02,383
We need chunking strategies that
preserve meaning, whether fixed

81
00:05:02,384 --> 00:05:05,503
size, semantic, or hi-hierarchical.

82
00:05:06,157 --> 00:05:09,550
And we need good metadata: who
created the document, when it

83
00:05:09,550 --> 00:05:13,375
was updated, who can access it,
and where the content came from.

84
00:05:13,706 --> 00:05:18,398
My practical advice, inspect the extracted
content before you tune retrieval.

85
00:05:18,729 --> 00:05:21,830
Otherwise, you're optimizing
against damaged evidence.

86
00:05:22,830 --> 00:05:28,523
The next two axes work closely together:
indexing and retrieval composition.

87
00:05:28,884 --> 00:05:32,906
Dense retrieval uses embeddings.

88
00:05:33,222 --> 00:05:36,568
It's very useful when the query
and the document express the

89
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:38,383
same idea in different words.

90
00:05:38,666 --> 00:05:42,855
Sparse retrieval, such as BM25, is
excellent for exact identifiers,

91
00:05:43,172 --> 00:05:44,861
product codes, and specific terms.

92
00:05:45,168 --> 00:05:48,108
Both are useful and both have weaknesses.

93
00:05:48,423 --> 00:05:52,483
That's why hybrid search is such
a common enterprise pattern, at

94
00:05:52,483 --> 00:05:54,017
least in the systems I studied.

95
00:05:54,321 --> 00:05:57,752
We collect candidates from multiple
strategies and then combine them.

96
00:05:58,443 --> 00:06:02,770
After that, a re-ranker can examine the
strongest candidates more carefully before

97
00:06:02,770 --> 00:06:04,556
they enter the model's context window.

98
00:06:04,877 --> 00:06:08,519
One important caution, though:
every additional stage has a

99
00:06:08,540 --> 00:06:12,914
cost in latency, infrastructure,
and operational complexity.

100
00:06:13,217 --> 00:06:16,975
So measure the end-to-end
experience for real user questions,

101
00:06:17,259 --> 00:06:18,935
not just retrieval accuracy.

102
00:06:19,251 --> 00:06:24,876
The best pipeline is the simplest one
that reliably meets your requirements.

103
00:06:25,876 --> 00:06:27,527
Let's talk about grounding.

104
00:06:27,862 --> 00:06:31,506
It's not enough for an
answer to sound convincing.

105
00:06:31,877 --> 00:06:35,457
An enterprise user should be able
to see where that answer came from.

106
00:06:35,802 --> 00:06:37,175
First, attribution.

107
00:06:37,522 --> 00:06:41,332
Connect important claims to the
specific document and evidence chunk.

108
00:06:41,696 --> 00:06:43,004
Second, faithfulness.

109
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:48,363
Evaluate whether the answer actually
follows from that evidence rather than

110
00:06:48,391 --> 00:06:51,047
introducing unsupported statements.

111
00:06:51,387 --> 00:06:53,226
And third, confidence signaling.

112
00:06:53,536 --> 00:06:58,210
When evidence is weak or missing,
make that limitation visible.

113
00:06:58,512 --> 00:07:03,153
For example, if the available
policy documents don't describe an

114
00:07:03,154 --> 00:07:07,889
exception, the assistant should say
the exception couldn't be verified.

115
00:07:08,174 --> 00:07:12,291
It shouldn't invent a policy
because the wording seems plausible.

116
00:07:12,613 --> 00:07:15,918
Citations also need to be
useful, not decorative.

117
00:07:16,259 --> 00:07:20,880
A link to a hundred-page PDF is far less
helpful than a precise reference to the

118
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:26,080
section that supports the claim Governance
is where many good research demos

119
00:07:26,102 --> 00:07:28,090
become difficult enterprise projects.

120
00:07:29,063 --> 00:07:31,001
I want to emphasize three capabilities.

121
00:07:31,318 --> 00:07:34,594
The first is access
control-aware retrieval.

122
00:07:34,921 --> 00:07:39,022
Security should be enforced when
candidates are selected, not only when a

123
00:07:39,022 --> 00:07:41,631
result is displayed in the user interface.

124
00:07:41,940 --> 00:07:43,668
The second is provenance.

125
00:07:44,329 --> 00:07:48,114
You need to trace from the answer
to the retrieved chunk to the

126
00:07:48,116 --> 00:07:49,943
source document and its version.

127
00:07:50,282 --> 00:07:53,436
That helps with audit
compliance and debugging.

128
00:07:53,804 --> 00:07:56,234
The third is time-aware evaluation.

129
00:07:56,590 --> 00:07:59,582
Sometimes an answer is semantically
correct, but based on a

130
00:07:59,582 --> 00:08:01,008
document that's no longer valid.

131
00:08:01,316 --> 00:08:05,013
Think of a pricing policy
that was revised last month.

132
00:08:05,364 --> 00:08:08,681
An outdated answer can be more
damaging than an obvious failure.

133
00:08:09,376 --> 00:08:14,073
These requirements must be part of the
retrieval architecture from the beginning.

134
00:08:14,417 --> 00:08:16,787
Retrofitting them later is much harder

135
00:08:17,787 --> 00:08:21,478
Even with these patterns,
several hard problems remain.

136
00:08:21,834 --> 00:08:27,264
There are seven open engineering
challenges, and here are the first four.

137
00:08:27,604 --> 00:08:30,133
Number one: long document grounding.

138
00:08:30,466 --> 00:08:34,026
Models may accept large context, but
tracing a specific claim across a

139
00:08:34,026 --> 00:08:35,758
long document is still challenging.

140
00:08:36,429 --> 00:08:40,409
Number two: heterogeneous
content retrieval.

141
00:08:40,738 --> 00:08:44,444
How do we rank a table, a
diagram, a code snippet, and

142
00:08:44,444 --> 00:08:46,314
a paragraph in a coherent way?

143
00:08:46,628 --> 00:08:51,033
Number three: knowledge freshness
through changed data capture.

144
00:08:51,393 --> 00:08:55,259
Enterprises need their search systems
to reflect updates and deletions

145
00:08:55,261 --> 00:08:59,064
from live sources, often with
complex consistency requirements.

146
00:08:59,368 --> 00:09:03,540
And number four: confidentiality
preserving evaluation.

147
00:09:03,856 --> 00:09:07,605
We want meaningful benchmarks, but we
can't simply export sensitive customer

148
00:09:07,607 --> 00:09:09,856
documents to a public evaluation system.

149
00:09:10,198 --> 00:09:11,709
These problems are closely linked.

150
00:09:12,393 --> 00:09:15,723
Better retrieval isn't just
about choosing a stronger model.

151
00:09:16,396 --> 00:09:22,523
It's about the architecture around the
model Here are three more challenges that

152
00:09:22,524 --> 00:09:24,915
become very visible at enterprise scale.

153
00:09:25,247 --> 00:09:28,571
Number five, multi-agent retrieval.

154
00:09:28,895 --> 00:09:32,939
When different agents search
independently, how do we avoid

155
00:09:32,957 --> 00:09:37,443
duplicated evidence, conflicting
context, or lost provenance?

156
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:41,168
Number six, the trade-off
between latency and cost.

157
00:09:41,466 --> 00:09:45,209
Multiple search passes and large
rerankers may increase accuracy,

158
00:09:45,473 --> 00:09:48,779
but they also increase tail
latency and infrastructure cost.

159
00:09:49,073 --> 00:09:53,271
And number seven, evaluation
under real enterprise conditions.

160
00:09:53,925 --> 00:09:57,478
Public benchmarks rarely capture
access controls, proprietary

161
00:09:57,482 --> 00:10:02,602
terminology, mixed document formats,
or production service level objectives.

162
00:10:02,935 --> 00:10:04,096
So what should we do?

163
00:10:04,437 --> 00:10:08,529
Build evaluation sets from real task
patterns with appropriate privacy

164
00:10:08,530 --> 00:10:11,537
controls and measure the complete system.

165
00:10:11,885 --> 00:10:14,959
A good offline score doesn't
automatically translate into a

166
00:10:14,959 --> 00:10:16,990
dependable enterprise experience.

167
00:10:17,990 --> 00:10:20,916
This slide summarizes an
important distinction.

168
00:10:21,258 --> 00:10:24,893
A research prototype may run against
public data, use static document

169
00:10:24,896 --> 00:10:26,831
snapshots, and serve a single user.

170
00:10:27,119 --> 00:10:30,660
That's perfectly reasonable
for testing an algorithm.

171
00:10:31,430 --> 00:10:34,942
An enterprise production system
is a different environment.

172
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:39,061
It may have tenant-specific
permissions, a complete audit

173
00:10:39,061 --> 00:10:44,178
requirement, confidential corpora,
and content that changes continuously.

174
00:10:44,838 --> 00:10:49,376
So the gap isn't primarily algorithmic,
it's operational, organizational,

175
00:10:49,716 --> 00:10:51,076
and sometimes regulatory.

176
00:10:51,414 --> 00:10:54,517
This also explains why
reproducing a benchmark result

177
00:10:54,545 --> 00:10:58,085
isn't the same as delivering a
successful production deployment.

178
00:10:58,709 --> 00:11:03,630
Yes, we need performance, but we also
need correctness over time, security

179
00:11:03,631 --> 00:11:08,414
boundaries, observability, and a clear
ownership model for the knowledge itself

180
00:11:09,414 --> 00:11:11,813
So what's the larger architectural shift?

181
00:11:12,159 --> 00:11:16,725
I believe we should stop viewing
RAG as only a pipeline that

182
00:11:16,725 --> 00:11:18,964
turns documents into embeddings.

183
00:11:19,272 --> 00:11:22,138
Instead, we should design
it as a knowledge system.

184
00:11:22,803 --> 00:11:26,666
That means treating source content
as governed data with schemas,

185
00:11:26,686 --> 00:11:30,751
versions, permissions, change
events, and traceable relationships.

186
00:11:31,052 --> 00:11:36,293
It means recognizing that the index is a
derived representation of that knowledge.

187
00:11:36,591 --> 00:11:40,276
It's not the source of truth, and
it means designing the experience

188
00:11:40,277 --> 00:11:43,203
around the questions users
are actually trying to answer.

189
00:11:43,525 --> 00:11:46,771
Once we take this view, many
decisions become clearer.

190
00:11:47,074 --> 00:11:51,802
We can reason about freshness,
lineage, access control, and evaluation

191
00:11:52,155 --> 00:11:53,780
as parts of one architecture.

192
00:11:54,047 --> 00:11:59,851
That's also how we keep enterprise
AI maintainable as models and

193
00:11:59,851 --> 00:12:02,551
retrieval technologies keep evolving.

194
00:12:03,551 --> 00:12:05,587
Let me bring the main ideas together.

195
00:12:05,912 --> 00:12:08,623
First, put context quality at the center.

196
00:12:09,430 --> 00:12:12,567
Prompt design matters, but it
can't compensate for poor evidence.

197
00:12:12,888 --> 00:12:19,193
Second, evaluate retrieval along all five
axes: ingestion, indexing, retrieval,

198
00:12:19,215 --> 00:12:22,437
composition, grounding, and governance.

199
00:12:22,736 --> 00:12:27,738
Third, treat access aware retrieval and
provenance as fundamental production

200
00:12:27,738 --> 00:12:29,891
requirements, not optional extras.

201
00:12:30,547 --> 00:12:32,440
And finally, stay vendor neutral.

202
00:12:33,137 --> 00:12:37,868
No single index type framework or model
is right for every enterprise workload.

203
00:12:38,169 --> 00:12:41,618
What matters is choosing a
system that fits your content,

204
00:12:41,906 --> 00:12:46,618
your users, your security model,
and your operational targets.

205
00:12:46,923 --> 00:12:50,201
If you take only one idea from
this session, let it be this.

206
00:12:50,492 --> 00:12:54,914
Improving an AI answer often starts
well before the prompt reaches the model

207
00:12:55,914 --> 00:12:59,703
Before we close, here are four concrete
steps you can take with your own system.

208
00:13:00,022 --> 00:13:01,548
First, audit ingestion.

209
00:13:02,258 --> 00:13:06,529
After an answer, look at the
actual extracted text, tables,

210
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:08,816
chunk boundaries, and metadata.

211
00:13:09,147 --> 00:13:13,217
Second, implement access control
at retrieval time and test it with

212
00:13:13,229 --> 00:13:15,010
users who have different permissions.

213
00:13:15,289 --> 00:13:17,008
Third, instrument provenance.

214
00:13:17,322 --> 00:13:21,160
Make it possible to trace every answer
back through the retrieved chunks

215
00:13:21,438 --> 00:13:23,576
to the original source and version.

216
00:13:23,926 --> 00:13:28,405
And fourth, build an evaluation suite
that reflects your enterprise conditions,

217
00:13:28,712 --> 00:13:31,185
not only a generic public benchmark.

218
00:13:31,690 --> 00:13:35,454
Start with a manageable set of real
questions and include the difficult cases:

219
00:13:35,722 --> 00:13:41,151
outdated documents, exact identifiers,
missing evidence, and restricted content.

220
00:13:41,477 --> 00:13:45,331
These steps are practical,
measurable, and useful, whichever

221
00:13:45,333 --> 00:13:47,298
model or vector store you've chosen.

222
00:13:48,298 --> 00:13:49,836
With that, I'll conclude.

223
00:13:50,157 --> 00:13:55,025
Thank you for joining me at Conf42
Prompt Engineering twenty twenty-six.

224
00:13:55,339 --> 00:13:59,453
I hope this framework helps you see
retrieval as a broader enterprise

225
00:13:59,453 --> 00:14:04,445
knowledge challenge and gives you a
practical way to prioritize improvements.

226
00:14:04,761 --> 00:14:05,893
I'm Ravi Ray.

227
00:14:06,212 --> 00:14:10,120
My LinkedIn link is on the screen,
and I'd be happy to continue

228
00:14:10,126 --> 00:14:11,643
the technical discussion there.

229
00:14:11,947 --> 00:14:14,479
Thank you, and have a great conference.

